 "caveats": [
  "Parity of the batch job with the study's cached features on Kang 2.1: Spearman prod 1.0, atlas_knn 0.9844, atlas_clf 0.927, ct_entropy 1.0, atlas_nnls 1.0.",
  "scDblFinder is ahead of Singlet on 8 of 17 datasets (pbmc-1A-dm, cline-ch, mkidney-ch, HEK-HMEC-MULTI, HMEC-orig-MULTI, HMEC-rep-MULTI, hm-6k, hm-12k); the pooled mean is a tie, not a win. The per-dataset gate is the previous Singlet scorer; the pooled and Kang-lane means gate against the best competitor with a tolerance from the bootstrap over datasets.",
  "Datasets where the previous Singlet scorer is ahead of the combiner: HEK-HMEC-MULTI (auprc), HMEC-rep-MULTI (auprc), mkidney-ch (auroc). These are the failing per-dataset metrics.",
  "Only experimentally visible doublets are labelled (different donors, hashtags, barcodes or species): same-donor homotypic doublets are invisible to the truth and to every method. About a quarter of cross-donor PBMC doublets look like one cell type; expression methods find roughly half of them, genotype doublets 0.89.",
  "The Xi & Li matrices come from other pipelines (Cell Ranger and others); the atlas network is tuned to Singlet's counts and gives a confident lineage to far fewer cells there (1-7% against 40% on Singlet's lane 2.1), so the gain on them is, if anything, understated. Lane 2.1 as Singlet counts it is the only set on Singlet's own counts.",
  "The 17 datasets include two sets of one Kang lane (2.1 on Singlet counts and as the Cell Ranger 1.0 matrix) and six Kang lanes in all, so the pooled mean weights the Kang study 6/17. Weighting each of the six studies once, scDblFinder is ahead (0.560 against 0.552); that gate has its own tolerance from the bootstrap over six studies, which is wide.",
  "The combiner was chosen after comparing feature sets on these same 17 datasets (bench/doublets/cheap_features.py), so the pooled numbers carry a small selection effect; adding Scrublet as a feature gave 0.563 pooled and 0.575 on the Kang lanes, below the shipped combiner's 0.585 there, and was not adopted."
 ]
}
